@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@cindex overview
@cindex two halves

An agent, a script, or you at a shell names a @dfn{verb}; Emacs answers
with one JSON @dfn{envelope} and a stable exit code.  There are two
halves:

@table @asis
@item The @command{etm} CLI (Rust)
Parses argv into a request, base64-wraps it (so nothing is ever quoted
as an Emacs Lisp string), and speaks the Emacs server socket protocol
directly, the one @command{emacsclient} speaks, falling back to
@samp{emacsclient --eval}.  A snapshot round trip takes about a
millisecond.

@item The @code{etm} Emacs package (@file{lisp/})
One entry point, @code{(etm-rpc @var{request-base64})}, dispatching a
closed verb table.  It loads in @samp{emacs -Q} and needs Emacs 29.1 or
newer.
@end table

@cindex workspace
@cindex pane
@cindex pane address
etm's vocabulary is small.  A @dfn{workspace} is whatever the active
backend calls one: a persp-mode perspective, a tabspaces or tab-bar tab,
or the one @samp{global} workspace of a session without any
(@pxref{Workspace Backends}).  A @dfn{pane} is a buffer-shaped resource
of one workspace: a shell, a running command, a file, a directory tree,
or a named @dfn{page}.  A pane is addressed
@samp{@var{workspace}/@var{pane-key}}, where the key is the caller's own
name for it (@samp{review/sh}, @samp{api/eng.2}); buffer names are only
labels.

@cindex closed verb table
The verb table is closed: an unknown verb is a typed usage error, never
an evaluation.  @code{eval} is the one escape hatch, and it is logged and
flagged (@pxref{Other Verbs}).  Handlers never signal past the
dispatcher: a failure is an envelope with @code{ok} false, because the
Emacs server answers an escaped error by stalling the user's session for
two seconds.

@cindex next hints
Every envelope carries @code{next}: the one to three commands that most
plausibly follow, so a caller reads its next step instead of working it
out.  Every call also leaves one event in Emacs (@pxref{Event Sinks}),
whose id the envelope cites, so diagnosing after the fact is a read.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation
@cindex installation

etm is two installable pieces.  The CLI is enough on its own: it carries
a copy of the Emacs package and loads it into the target Emacs on first
use (@pxref{The CLI}).  Install the Emacs package as well when you want
to configure etm in your init file or call it from Lisp.

@menu
* The CLI::                     cargo install.
* The Emacs Package::           MELPA, package-vc-install, straight.
* Building This Manual::        makeinfo and install-info.
@end menu

@node The CLI
@section The CLI
@cindex cargo install
@cindex autoload of the Emacs package

@example
cargo install etm                 # the CLI, with the elisp bundled
etm install-elisp ~/.emacs.d/etm  # optional: put the package on disk
@end example

Emacs must run a server: @kbd{M-x server-start}, or start it as
@samp{emacs --daemon}.  Pick the server with @option{--socket
@var{name-or-path}}; without it etm reads @env{ETM_SOCKET}, then
@env{EMACS_SOCKET_NAME}, then uses @samp{server} in the usual socket
directory.

@cindex @file{etm.el}, finding
@vindex ETM_RPC_EL
You do not have to install the Emacs package.  When the target Emacs has
not loaded etm, the CLI loads it, from the first of:

@enumerate
@item
@option{--rpc-el @var{path}}: @file{etm.el}, its directory, or your own
configuration file that loads it;
@item
@env{ETM_RPC_EL}, the same;
@item
the server's own installation, @code{(locate-library "etm")};
@item
the copy bundled in the binary, written to
@file{~/.cache/etm/lisp-@var{version}-@var{hash}/}.
@end enumerate

@option{--no-autoload} never loads anything; a request to an Emacs
without etm then fails.

@node The Emacs Package
@section The Emacs Package
@cindex MELPA
@cindex package-vc-install
@cindex straight.el

The package lives in @file{lisp/} of the repository, with this manual in
@file{docs/etm.texi}.

@subheading From MELPA

@lisp
(package-install 'etm)
@end lisp

@noindent
The MELPA recipe builds this manual too:

@lisp
(etm :fetcher github :repo "davidawad/etm"
     :files (:defaults "docs/etm.texi"))
@end lisp

@subheading With @code{package-vc-install} (Emacs 29.1 and newer)

@lisp
(package-vc-install
 '(etm :url "@value{URL}"
       :lisp-dir "lisp"
       :doc "docs/etm.texi"))
@end lisp

@noindent
On Emacs 30, @code{use-package} takes the same specification:

@lisp
(use-package etm
  :vc (:url "@value{URL}" :lisp-dir "lisp" :doc "docs/etm.texi"))
@end lisp

@subheading With straight.el

@lisp
(straight-use-package
 '(etm :host github :repo "davidawad/etm"
       :files ("lisp/*.el" "docs/etm.texi")))
@end lisp

@subheading From a checkout

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/etm/lisp")
(require 'etm)
@end lisp

@node Building This Manual
@section Building This Manual
@cindex Info manual, building
@cindex makeinfo

Package managers build the Info file for you.  From a checkout:

@example
makeinfo --no-split -o docs/etm.info docs/etm.texi
install-info docs/etm.info docs/dir
@end example

@noindent
then add @file{docs/} to @code{Info-additional-directory-list}, and
@kbd{C-h i} lists ``etm''.  The repository's CI builds the manual and
fails on any warning.

@node Quick Start
@chapter Quick Start
@cindex quick start

Start an Emacs server, then drive it from a terminal:

@example
$ emacs --daemon
$ etm ws new review --switch
$ etm pane new review sh --kind shell --region bottom
$ etm send review/sh 'make test' --enter
$ etm wait review/sh --until match:'passed\|failed' --timeout 5m
$ etm capture review/sh --since c1:@dots{}
@end example

@noindent
Each answer is one envelope (@pxref{The Result Envelope}).  Off a
terminal, or with @option{--json}, it is JSON:

@example
$ etm --json send review/sh 'echo hi' --enter
@{"schema": "etm.result/1", "ok": true, "verb": "send",
 "data": @{"addr": "review/sh", "chars": 7, "enter": true,
          "cursor": "c1:ea29@dots{}:33"@},
 "rev": null, "warnings": [], "errors": [],
 "next": ["etm wait review/sh --until idle",
          "etm capture review/sh --since c1:ea29@dots{}:33",
          "etm wait review/sh --until match:REGEXP --since c1:ea29@dots{}:33"],
 "events": ["ev_1a105d65cae0003"]@}
@end example

@cindex capture cursor
The @code{cursor} that @code{send} returns marks the pane's end before
the text went in.  Pass it to @code{capture --since} to read only what
arrived after it, and to @code{wait --until match:@var{re} --since} to
match output the command has already printed.  Without @option{--since},
@code{wait} searches only output that arrives after the wait begins.

A @samp{global} workspace always exists, so the shortest session needs
no workspace at all:

@example
$ etm pane new global sh --kind shell
$ etm send global/sh 'ls' --enter
$ etm capture global/sh --lines 20
@end example

@noindent
Use @samp{etm capabilities} for what this Emacs supports, and
@samp{etm doctor} when something looks wrong.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration
@cindex configuration
@cindex customization group

Every option is in the customization group @code{etm}: @kbd{M-x
customize-group @key{RET} etm @key{RET}}.  None is needed in a bare
@samp{emacs -Q}.

@section Workspaces

@defopt etm-workspace-backend
Which notion of workspace etm drives: @code{auto} (the default),
@code{persp-mode}, @code{tabspaces}, @code{tab-bar}, @code{single}, or
the name of a backend you wrote (@pxref{Writing a Backend}).  @code{auto}
picks the first available backend in
@code{etm-workspace-backend-candidates} on every request, so turning on
@code{persp-mode} or @code{tabspaces-mode} later takes effect without a
restart.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-workspace-backend-candidates
The backends @code{auto} tries, in order.  Default:
@code{(persp-mode tabspaces tab-bar single)}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-persp-id-parameter
The perspective parameter that holds a perspective's stable etm id.
Default: @code{etm-ws-id}.  Point it at a parameter another package
already keeps a stable id in, to share that id.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-tag-parameters
Which workspace parameter holds each tag a controller mounts a
workspace with: an alist @code{(@var{tag} . @var{parameter})}.
@xref{Workspace Tags}.
@end defopt

@section Panes and layout

@defopt etm-pane-buffer-name-function
Turns a pane's base label (@samp{*etm:@var{ws}/@var{key}*}) into a new
buffer's name.  Default: @code{generate-new-buffer-name}.  The name is
only what a person sees; identity comes from
@code{etm-resource-resolver}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-resource-resolver
The functions that resolve pane identity, a plist, or @code{nil} for
etm's own registry.  @xref{Pane Identity}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-layout-dock-function
The function that shows a pane in a side region.  Default:
@code{etm-layout-dock-side-window}.  @xref{Docking}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-side-window-size
Width or height of a docked pane's side window, as a fraction of the
frame, for the default dock function.  Default: @code{0.3}.
@end defopt

@section Pages

@defopt etm-page-types
Registered page types, an alist @code{(@var{name} . @var{function})}.
@xref{Page Types}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-page-type-functions
Resolvers for page names @code{etm-page-types} does not list, each
called with the name and returning a page function or @code{nil}.
Default: @code{(etm-page-command-type)}, which accepts any interactive
command of that name.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-page-delay
Seconds after @code{pane.new} answers before its page renders.  Default:
@code{0}.  The render runs on a timer either way, outside the server's
process filter.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-page-slow-ms
A render that took longer than this many milliseconds fails the doctor
row @samp{etm.pages-responsive}.  Default: @code{2000}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-page-stuck-seconds
A page still rendering after this many seconds fails the doctor row
@samp{etm.pages}.  Default: @code{60}.
@end defopt

@section Events, doctor and snapshot

@defopt etm-event-ring-size
How many events etm's own ring keeps.  Default: @code{200}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-event-log-function
The function that records one event.  Default:
@code{etm-event-ring-log}.  @xref{Event Sinks}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-event-list-function
The function returning every retained event, oldest first.  Default:
@code{etm-event-ring-list}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-event-functions
A hook of sinks, each called with every event after it is recorded.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-doctor-functions
A hook of functions adding rows to @samp{etm doctor}.
@xref{Doctor Checks}.
@end defopt

@defopt etm-snapshot-functions
A hook of functions adding sections to @samp{etm snapshot}.
@xref{Snapshot Sections}.
@end defopt

@section Safety

@defopt etm-allow-eval
Non-@code{nil} (the default) lets the @code{eval} verb run arbitrary
forms.  Set it to @code{nil} to refuse them with
@samp{capability.disabled}.  Anyone who can reach the server socket can
already evaluate Lisp through @command{emacsclient}; this option only
closes etm's own door.
@end defopt

@node Workspace Backends
@chapter Workspace Backends
@cindex workspace backend
@cindex backend

Emacs has several notions of ``workspace'', and none at all in a bare
session.  etm drives all of them through one protocol, and
@code{etm-workspace-backend} picks which:

@multitable @columnfractions .17 .33 .50
@headitem Backend @tab A workspace is @tab Available when
@item @code{persp-mode}
@tab a perspective
@tab @code{persp-mode} is on
@item @code{tabspaces}
@tab a tab with its own buffer list
@tab @code{tabspaces-mode} is on
@item @code{tab-bar}
@tab a tab-bar tab
@tab @code{tab-bar-mode} is on, or the frame has more than one tab
@item @code{single}
@tab the one @samp{global} workspace
@tab always
@end multitable

@cindex persp-mode
@noindent
@strong{persp-mode.}  A workspace is a perspective; its stable id lives
in the perspective parameter named by @code{etm-persp-id-parameter}.
persp-mode ignores a daemon's own frame, so @code{ws switch} picks a frame
persp-mode manages, and fails with @samp{capability.unavailable} when
there is none (open one with @samp{emacsclient -t} or @samp{-c}).

@cindex tab-bar
@noindent
@strong{tab-bar.}  A workspace is a tab of the selected frame.  The
handle is an id etm stores in the tab itself (its @code{etm-id} entry);
workspace parameters live in the tab under @code{etm-params}.  Membership
is the tab's buffer list.  The frame's only tab cannot be killed.

@cindex tabspaces
@noindent
@strong{tabspaces.}  The tab-bar backend with tabspaces' notion of
buffer membership.

@cindex single workspace
@noindent
@strong{single.}  One workspace, @samp{global}, holding every buffer.
@code{ws new}, @code{switch}, @code{rename} and @code{kill} fail with
@samp{capability.unavailable}; panes work as everywhere else.

@cindex workspace id
Pane identity keys on the workspace's @emph{id}, not its name, so
@code{ws rename} keeps every pane of the workspace.

