@node Pane Identity
@section Pane Identity
@cindex resolver
@cindex pane identity
@cindex registry of panes

A pane @emph{is} the buffer registered under the resource key
@code{etm-pane:@var{key}} in its workspace's row, so the same key in two
workspaces is two buffers.  By default etm keeps that registry itself (a
weak hash table from @code{(@var{workspace-id} . @var{key})} to buffer).
A configuration with its own buffer namespace hands etm its functions
instead, through @code{etm-resource-resolver}, a plist whose keys each
replace one step:

@table @code
@item :peek (@var{key} @var{ws})
The live buffer registered as @var{key} in @var{ws}, or @code{nil}.
@item :get (@var{key} @var{create} @var{ws})
That buffer, made by calling @var{create} (no arguments) and registered
when absent.
@item :replace (@var{old} @var{new})
Register @var{new} in @var{old}'s row; return @var{new}.  Page panes use
this when the page's buffer takes over from its placeholder.
@item :buffer-id (@var{buffer})
A stable id of @var{buffer} as an object.  Capture cursors key on it.
@item :owned (@var{ws})
Live buffers owned by @var{ws}.
@item :registered-p (@var{buffer})
Non-@code{nil} when @var{buffer} is registered anywhere.
@item :snapshot ()
A plist describing the registry, shown as @code{namespace} in
@samp{etm snapshot}.
@end table

@var{key} is a symbol and @var{ws} an @code{etm-ws} structure (accessors
@code{etm-ws-name}, @code{etm-ws-handle} and @code{etm-ws-id}).  A step
left out keeps etm's default, which is also callable:

@defun etm-resource-default-peek key ws
@defunx etm-resource-default-get key create ws
@defunx etm-resource-default-replace old new
@defunx etm-resource-default-buffer-id buffer
@defunx etm-resource-default-owned ws
@defunx etm-resource-default-snapshot
etm's own registry, used for every step @code{etm-resource-resolver}
does not name.
@end defun

@noindent
etm calls the resolver through these:

@defun etm-resource-peek key ws
@defunx etm-resource-get key create ws
@defunx etm-resource-replace old new
@defunx etm-resource-buffer-id buffer
@defunx etm-resource-owned ws
@defunx etm-resource-registered-p buffer
@defunx etm-resource-snapshot
The resolver's step, or etm's default.
@end defun

@vindex etm-pane-key
@vindex etm-pane-kind
@vindex etm-pane-region
@vindex etm-pane-proxy
Whatever the resolver, a pane buffer carries the buffer-local tags
@code{etm-pane-key}, @code{etm-pane-kind}, @code{etm-pane-region} and
@code{etm-pane-proxy}, and a window showing it has the window parameter
@code{etm-pane}.

@node Docking
@section Docking
@cindex docking
@cindex region

A pane may ask for a @dfn{region}: @code{main}, @code{left},
@code{right}, @code{bottom} or @code{top}.  @code{main} is the ordinary
window area.  The side regions go through
@code{etm-layout-dock-function}, called with the buffer and the region
as a symbol, returning the window it used (or @code{nil}).  @code{top}
docks at the bottom, with a @samp{degraded.region_top} warning.  A
region asked for in a workspace that is not shown applies on the next
@code{pane focus} (@samp{layout.deferred}).

@defun etm-layout-dock-side-window buffer region
The default: @code{display-buffer-in-side-window} at @var{region}, sized
by @code{etm-side-window-size}.
@end defun

@node Page Types
@section Page Types
@cindex page
@cindex page type
@findex etm-page-args

A @code{page} pane shows a named view: a function that displays a
buffer (a dashboard, an agenda, a board).  @samp{--page @var{name}}
resolves through @code{etm-page-types}, then the resolvers in
@code{etm-page-type-functions}; with neither it is
@samp{capability.unavailable}.

The function is called interactively when it is a command, and with no
arguments otherwise.  A page function that needs arguments declares how
to get them from the workspace: its symbol property @code{etm-page-args}
holds a function of the plist @code{(:subject @var{s} :dir @var{d})}
returning the argument list.

@cindex page rendering
Running a page inside @code{pane.new} would run it inside the server's
process filter, where a page that prompts, or fetches synchronously,
wedges the whole server.  So @code{pane.new} answers at once with a
placeholder buffer as the pane (@code{page_state} @code{"rendering"}),
and the page renders on a timer.  The render binds
@code{inhibit-interaction}, so a page that would prompt fails as
@samp{page.needs_input}; one that shows no buffer fails as
@samp{page.no_buffer}.  When it succeeds, the page's buffer takes over
the pane (registry row, windows, tags) and @code{page_state} becomes
@code{"ready"}, with @code{page_ms}.  A failed render leaves the
placeholder holding the error.

@defun etm-page-command-type name
The default resolver: the interactive command named @var{name}, or
@code{nil}.
@end defun

@defun etm-page-function name
The page function for @var{name}; signals @samp{capability.unavailable}
when there is none.
@end defun

@defun etm-page-names
The names registered in @code{etm-page-types}, as @samp{etm
capabilities} lists them under @code{pages}.
@end defun

@node Doctor Checks
@section Doctor Checks
@cindex doctor

@samp{etm doctor} runs etm's own probes (@samp{etm.server},
@samp{etm.json}, @samp{etm.workspaces}, @samp{etm.panes-registered},
@samp{etm.pages}, @samp{etm.pages-responsive}), then every function in
@code{etm-doctor-functions}.  Each is called with no arguments and
returns a list of rows, each a plist @code{(:check @var{name} :status
@var{status} :hint @var{text})}, @var{status} being @code{pass},
@code{fail} or @code{skip} (a symbol or a string).  A failing row
becomes a @samp{doctor.@var{check}} warning on the envelope and makes
@code{healthy} false.  A function that signals becomes a failing row
named after it.

@defun etm-probe check ok hint &optional skip
One row: @var{check} passes when @var{ok}, else fails, or skips when
@var{skip}.
@end defun

@node Snapshot Sections
@section Snapshot Sections
@cindex snapshot sections

Each function in @code{etm-snapshot-functions} is called with no
arguments and returns a plist merged into @samp{etm snapshot}'s data.
A key etm already uses keeps etm's value.

@node Event Sinks
@section Event Sinks
@cindex events
@cindex event sink
@cindex event ring

Every call leaves one event (a quiet @code{wait} poll excepted), and
the envelope's @code{events} cites its id.  An event is a plist with at
least @code{:id} (a string sorting by time, @samp{ev_@dots{}}),
@code{:ts} (float seconds), @code{:level} (@code{debug}, @code{info},
@code{warn} or @code{error}) and @code{:message}.  Successful calls log
at @code{debug}, typed errors and @code{eval} at @code{info}, and only
@samp{internal.error} (a handler bug) at @code{warn}.

@code{etm-event-log-function} records an event (called with
@var{level} and @var{message}, returning the plist) and
@code{etm-event-list-function} returns them all, oldest first; replace
both to keep events in your own store.  To observe events without
replacing the store, add a sink to @code{etm-event-functions}.

@defun etm-event-log level format-string &rest args
Record one event at @var{level}, its message formatted from
@var{format-string} and @var{args}; run the sinks; return the plist.
Your own code may log through it too.
@end defun

@defun etm-event-list
Every retained event, oldest first.
@end defun

@defun etm-event-ring-log level message
@defunx etm-event-ring-list
The default store: a ring of @code{etm-event-ring-size} events.
@end defun

@node Workspace Tags
@section Workspace Tags
@cindex tags
@cindex managed workspace

A controller that mounts workspaces through etm tags them, and reads
the tags back from the live session instead of keeping its own
database.  @code{etm-tag-parameters} names the workspace parameter each
tag lives in:

@table @code
@item tag
The name the workspace was mounted under.  A workspace with this tag is
@code{managed} in @samp{ws ls}.
@item subject
What the workspace is about (@samp{ws new --subject}).
@item spec
The revision it was rendered from (@samp{ws new --spec}).
@item owner
The session that created it (@samp{ws new --owner}); set once.
@item root
Its directory, where new panes start.
@end table

@noindent
Point them at other parameter names to share tags with an existing
setup.

@defun etm-tag-parameter tag
The workspace parameter for @var{tag}.
@end defun

@node Writing a Backend
@section Writing a Backend
@cindex writing a backend
@cindex backend protocol

A backend is a set of @code{cl-defmethod}s on the @code{etm-backend-*}
generic functions, specialized on @code{(eql '@var{name})}.  Every
method takes the backend symbol first; a @var{handle} is whatever the
backend uses to reach a workspace (a perspective object, a tab id).

@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-available-p backend
Non-@code{nil} when @var{backend} can be used now.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-list backend
Handles of every workspace, in the backend's own order.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-current backend
The handle of the selected frame's workspace.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-name backend handle
Its display name.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-id backend handle
Its stable id, which must survive renames.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-get backend name
The handle named @var{name}, or @code{nil}.  The default searches
@code{etm-backend-list}.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-mutable-p backend
Non-@code{nil} (the default) when the backend can create, switch,
rename and kill workspaces.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-real-p backend handle
Non-@code{nil} (the default) unless @var{handle} cannot be renamed or
killed.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-new backend name
Create workspace @var{name} without showing it; return its handle.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-switch backend handle
Show it in the selected frame.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-rename backend handle new-name
Rename it.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-kill backend handle
Remove it; its buffers stay alive (etm reaps pane buffers itself).
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-buffers backend handle
The buffers that belong to it.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-add-buffer backend handle buffer
Make @var{buffer} a member.  The default does nothing.
@end deffn
@deffn {Generic Function} etm-backend-parameter backend handle param
@deffnx {Generic Function} etm-backend-set-parameter backend handle param value
Read and write a parameter stored on the workspace, where tags live.
@end deffn

@noindent
Add the backend's name to @code{etm-workspace-backend-candidates} for
@code{auto} to find it, or set @code{etm-workspace-backend} to it.

@defun etm-backend
The backend symbol in effect for this request.
@end defun

@defun etm-backend-require-available backend
Signal @samp{capability.unavailable} unless @var{backend} is usable now.
@end defun

@node Worked Example
@section Worked Example
@cindex worked example
@cindex example configuration

This configuration keeps etm's own registry and backends, and uses the
other extension points: docked panes open in the direction asked for
rather than in side windows, two page types are registered, one doctor
row is added, and every event is appended to a JSON-lines file.

@lisp
(require 'etm)

;; Docking: an ordinary window in the region's direction.
(setq etm-layout-dock-function
      (lambda (buffer region)
        (display-buffer
         buffer `(display-buffer-in-direction (direction . ,region)))))

;; Pages: a command, and a function that takes the workspace's root.
(add-to-list 'etm-page-types '("agenda" . org-agenda-list))

(defun my-project-notes (dir)
  "Show NOTES.org of DIR."
  (find-file (expand-file-name "NOTES.org" dir)))
(put 'my-project-notes 'etm-page-args
     (lambda (ws) (list (plist-get ws :dir))))
(add-to-list 'etm-page-types '("notes" . my-project-notes))

;; Doctor: one extra row.
(defun my-etm-doctor-git ()
  "Doctor row: git is on `exec-path'."
  (list (list :check "my.git"
              :status (if (executable-find "git") 'pass 'fail)
              :hint "git on exec-path (cmd panes run it)")))
(add-hook 'etm-doctor-functions #'my-etm-doctor-git)

;; Events: append every event to a file as it happens.
(defun my-etm-event-to-file (event)
  "Append EVENT to ~/etm-events.jsonl."
  (let ((coding-system-for-write 'utf-8))
    (write-region
     (concat (json-serialize
              (list :id (plist-get event :id)
                    :ts (plist-get event :ts)
                    :level (symbol-name (plist-get event :level))
                    :message (plist-get event :message)))
             "\n")
     nil (expand-file-name "~/etm-events.jsonl") t 'silent)))
(add-hook 'etm-event-functions #'my-etm-event-to-file)
@end lisp

@noindent
Then, from a shell:

@example
$ etm ws new api --switch
$ etm pane new api notes --kind page --page notes --path ~/src/api --region right
$ etm pane ls api --fields addr,kind,page_state
$ etm doctor
$ tail -3 ~/etm-events.jsonl
@end example

@node Verb Reference
@chapter Verb Reference
@cindex verbs

Each verb below is shown as the CLI spells it.  Its name in the
envelope (and on the wire) is dotted: @samp{etm ws new} is
@code{ws.new}.  @samp{etm capabilities} lists the table this Emacs
answers, with each verb's mode (@code{read} or @code{write}).  The
@option{--json} examples are trimmed to the interesting fields.

@menu
* Observation Verbs::           snapshot, capabilities, doctor, events.
* Workspace Verbs::             ws ls, get, new, switch, rename, kill.
* Pane Verbs::                  pane ls, new, kill, focus.
* Pane I/O Verbs::              send, capture, wait.
* Other Verbs::                 eval, bench, install-elisp, help.
* Calling Verbs from Lisp::     @code{etm-rpc} and @code{etm-rpc-json}.
@end menu

@node Observation Verbs
@section Observation Verbs

@table @asis
@item @samp{etm snapshot [--fields @var{a},@var{b}]}
@cindex snapshot
The whole session in one read: @code{emacs} (version, pid, daemon,
server), @code{backend}, @code{current} workspace, @code{workspaces},
@code{panes}, the selected frame's @code{windows}, @code{namespace}
(the resolver's registry), the last ten @code{events}, plus any
@code{etm-snapshot-functions} sections.  @option{--fields} keeps only
the named top-level keys.

@item @samp{etm capabilities}
@cindex capabilities
Self-description: @code{verbs} (each with @code{mode} and
@code{summary}), @code{planned} verbs, pane @code{kinds}, @code{regions},
shell @code{backends}, the @code{workspaces} backend in effect and the
candidates, the dock function, registered @code{pages}, whether
@code{eval} is allowed, and the CLI's @code{global_flags} and per-verb
@code{modifiers}.

@item @samp{etm doctor}
@cindex doctor
Invariant probes.  @xref{Doctor Checks}.

@example
@{"ok": true, "verb": "doctor",
 "data": @{"healthy": true, "rows": [
   @{"check": "etm.server", "status": "pass",
    "hint": "Emacs server running (what the etm CLI connects to)"@},
   @{"check": "etm.workspaces", "status": "skip",
    "hint": "workspace backend: single (single = one global workspace)"@},
   @dots{}]@}@}
@end example

@item @samp{etm events [--since @var{t}|@var{event-id}] [--limit @var{n}] [--offset @var{n}]}
@cindex events verb
Events, oldest first, after @var{t} (epoch seconds) or after an event
id; at most @var{n} (default 50), the newest kept.  @option{--offset}
skips the newest @var{n}.  @code{data} is @code{events},
@code{dropped} (how many older ones the limit left out) and
@code{next_since}, the id to pass as the next @option{--since}.
@end table

@node Workspace Verbs
@section Workspace Verbs
@cindex workspace verbs

@table @asis
@item @samp{etm ws ls [--limit @var{n}] [--offset @var{n}]}
Every workspace, as records:

@example
@{"ok": true, "verb": "ws.ls",
 "data": [@{"name": "global", "id": "global", "current": true,
           "managed": false, "subject": null, "tag": null, "spec": null,
           "owner": null, "root": null, "panes": 0@}],
 "rev": "sha1:62f7fd6a0da68225"@}
@end example

@item @samp{etm ws get [@var{w}]}
One workspace (default: the current one) with its @code{pane_list}.

@item @samp{etm ws new @var{w} [--subject @var{s}] [--switch] [--spec @var{r}] [--owner @var{o}] [--adopt @var{from}]}
@cindex idempotent
Create workspace @var{w} without showing it (@option{--switch} shows
it).  Idempotent: an existing @var{w} is returned with @code{created}
false.  @option{--subject}, @option{--spec} and @option{--owner} set
those tags (@pxref{Workspace Tags}); the owner is never replaced.
@option{--adopt @var{from}} takes over an existing untagged workspace
as @var{w} instead: it is renamed and tagged, and its panes keep their
keys.  A name may not contain @samp{/}.

@item @samp{etm ws switch @var{w}}
Show @var{w} in the selected frame.

@item @samp{etm ws rename @var{w} @var{name}}
Relabel @var{w}.  Its id, and so every pane, is unchanged.
@samp{conflict.exists} when @var{name} is taken.

@item @samp{etm ws kill @var{w}}
Kill @var{w}'s pane buffers (except those shown outside it), then the
workspace.
@end table

@node Pane Verbs
@section Pane Verbs
@cindex pane verbs
@cindex pane kinds

@table @asis
@item @samp{etm pane ls [@var{w}|--all] [--limit @var{n}] [--offset @var{n}]}
The panes of @var{w} (default: the current workspace), or of every
workspace.  A pane record:

@example
@{"addr": "global/sh", "ws": "global", "ws_id": "global", "key": "sh",
 "kind": "shell", "region": null, "buffer": "*etm:global/sh*",
 "buffer_id": "ea2998841d0afd589e96536b00e918f9", "mode": "eshell-mode",
 "process": null, "pid": null, "proxy": null, "running": false,
 "visible": false, "size": 47, "idle_ms": 3019,
 "cursor": "c1:ea2998841d0afd589e96536b00e918f9:48"@}
@end example

@noindent
A page pane adds @code{page_state}, @code{page_ms} and
@code{page_error}.

@item @samp{etm pane new @var{w} @var{key} --kind @var{kind} [--region @var{r}] [--path @var{p}] [--cmd @var{c}] [--page @var{name}] [--backend @var{b}]}
Create pane @var{key} in @var{w}.  Idempotent: an existing pane of the
same kind is returned with @code{created} false; the same key with
another kind is @samp{conflict.kind_mismatch}.  A pane starts in
@option{--path}, else the workspace's root, else @file{~}.  Kinds:

@table @code
@item shell
An interactive shell: eshell, or eat with @option{--backend eat}.
@item cmd
A long-running command, @option{--cmd}, in comint; @option{--backend
eat} runs it in a terminal (comint with a @samp{degraded.eat_unavailable}
warning when eat is not installed).
@item editor
The file at @option{--path}, as an indirect buffer of its own, so two
workspaces never share one pane buffer.
@item tree
A fresh Dired buffer of the directory.
@item page
A registered page, @option{--page @var{name}} (@pxref{Page Types}).
@end table

@noindent
@code{agent} is refused as @samp{capability.unsupported_kind}: run an
agent's CLI as a @code{cmd} pane.

@item @samp{etm pane kill @var{addr}}
Kill the pane, and its process without asking.

@item @samp{etm pane focus @var{addr}}
Switch to the pane's workspace and select a window showing it, docking
it in its region when it has one.
@end table

@node Pane I/O Verbs
@section Pane I/O Verbs
@cindex send
@cindex capture
@cindex wait

@table @asis
@item @samp{etm send @var{addr} @var{text}|- [--enter]}
Type @var{text} (@samp{-} reads standard input) into a terminal pane:
eshell, eat, vterm, comint, term, or any buffer with a process.
@option{--enter} submits it.  @samp{pane.not_terminal} for a pane with
neither.  @code{data.cursor} is the pane's end before the text went in.

@item @samp{etm capture @var{addr} [--since @var{cursor}] [--lines @var{n}] [--max-chars @var{n}]}
The pane's text after @var{cursor} (default: from the start), cut to the
last @var{n} lines and the last @var{n} characters (default 262144), and
the @code{cursor} after it:

@example
@{"ok": true, "verb": "capture",
 "data": @{"addr": "global/sh", "text": "~ $ echo hi\nhi\n~ $ ",
          "from": 1, "to": 48, "truncated": true,
          "cursor": "c1:ea2998841d0afd589e96536b00e918f9:48"@}@}
@end example

@cindex cursor reset
A cursor from another buffer object (the pane was recreated), or past
the end (the buffer was cleared), restarts from the beginning with a
@samp{capture.cursor_reset} warning.

@item @samp{etm wait @var{addr} --until idle|exit|match:@var{re} [--timeout @var{dur}] [--idle-ms @var{n}] [--since @var{cursor}] [--poll-ms @var{n}]}
Block until the condition holds, or fail with @samp{wait.timeout} (exit
7) after @var{dur} (default 30s).  @code{idle}: no change for
@option{--idle-ms} (default 1000).  @code{exit}: no live foreground
process, with @code{exit_status}.  @code{match:@var{re}}: the Emacs
regexp @var{re} (alternation is @samp{\|}, a bare @samp{|} is literal) matches output after @option{--since} (default: the end
when the wait began), with @code{match} and the @code{cursor} after it.
The CLI polls every @option{--poll-ms} with a zero timeout, so Emacs is
never blocked; polls that decide nothing leave no event.  The timeout
envelope still carries the @code{cursor}:

@example
@{"ok": false, "verb": "wait",
 "data": @{"addr": "global/sh", "until": "match:hi", "elapsed_ms": 1961,
          "cursor": "c1:ea2998841d0afd589e96536b00e918f9:48"@},
 "errors": [@{"code": "wait.timeout",
             "msg": "`match:hi` not met for global/sh within 2.0s",
             "hint": null, "surface": "emacs", "retryable": true@}]@}
@end example
@end table

@node Other Verbs
@section Other Verbs
@cindex eval
@cindex escape hatch

@table @asis
@item @samp{etm eval @var{form}}
The escape hatch: evaluate @var{form} and return its printed
@code{value}.  It is logged as an @code{info} event, carries an
@samp{eval.escape_hatch} warning, and is never offered in @code{next}.
Prefer a verb; ask for one when none fits.  @code{etm-allow-eval} turns
it off.

@item @samp{etm bench [--n @var{n}]}
@cindex bench
Time @var{n} (default 50) snapshot round trips: @code{p50_ms},
@code{p95_ms} and the rest.

@item @samp{etm install-elisp @var{dir}}
Write the bundled Emacs package into @var{dir}.

@item @samp{etm help}, @samp{etm --help}
The verbs and flags in brief, pointing here.  @samp{etm --version}
prints the version.
@end table

@node Calling Verbs from Lisp
@section Calling Verbs from Lisp
@cindex calling from Lisp

@defun etm-rpc request-base64
The entry point the CLI calls: @var{request-base64} is base64 of UTF-8
JSON (@pxref{Wire Protocol}); the value is base64 of the envelope.
Never signals.
@end defun

@defun etm-rpc-json request-json
The same call with plain JSON strings, for callers inside Emacs:

@lisp
(etm-rpc-json "@{\"v\":1,\"verb\":\"ws.ls\",\"args\":@{@}@}")
@end lisp
@end defun

@defun etm-dispatch request
Run @var{request}, a parsed plist (@code{:v}, @code{:verb},
@code{:args}), through the verb table; return the envelope as a plist.
@end defun

@defvar etm-verbs
The closed verb table: @code{(@var{verb} @var{handler} @var{mode}
@var{summary})} entries.
@end defvar

@node The Result Envelope
@chapter The Result Envelope
@cindex envelope
@cindex etm.result/1

Every answer is one JSON object, schema @code{etm.result/1}:

@table @code
@item schema
Always @code{"etm.result/1"}.
@item ok
@code{true}, or @code{false} with at least one entry in @code{errors}.
@item verb
The dotted verb.
@item data
The verb's answer; on failure, partial data or @code{null}.
@item rev
For @code{snapshot}, @code{ws.ls} and @code{pane.ls}: a digest of the
observed workspaces and panes (@samp{sha1:@dots{}}), so a caller can tell
whether anything changed between two reads.  Otherwise @code{null}.
@item warnings
Entries @code{@{code, msg, @dots{}@}}: something worked, but not quite as
asked.
@item errors
Entries @code{@{code, msg, hint, surface, retryable@}}.
@item next
At most three commands that plausibly follow.
@item events
The id of the event this call left, when it left one.
@item elapsed_ms
Time spent inside Emacs.
@end table

@cindex error codes
@cindex exit codes
Error codes are dotted, and their family picks the exit code:

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .40 .48
@headitem Exit @tab Means @tab Codes
@item 0 @tab ok @tab
@item 1 @tab failure @tab any other code: @samp{capability.*}, @samp{conflict.*}, @samp{page.*}, @samp{pane.not_terminal}, @samp{transport.timeout}, @samp{internal.error}
@item 2 @tab usage @tab @samp{usage.*}
@item 3 @tab not found @tab @samp{not_found.*}
@item 4 @tab rev conflict @tab @samp{rev.*}
@item 5 @tab unreachable @tab @samp{*.unreachable}, such as @samp{driver.unreachable}
@item 6 @tab partial apply @tab @samp{apply.partial}
@item 7 @tab wait timeout @tab @samp{wait.timeout}
@end multitable

The codes etm itself uses:

@table @samp
@item usage.argv
The command line did not parse (the CLI's own check).
@item usage.bad_request
@itemx usage.protocol
@itemx usage.unknown_verb
The request was not base64 UTF-8 JSON, spoke another protocol version,
or named no verb in the table.
@item usage.missing_arg
@itemx usage.bad_arg
@itemx usage.bad_addr
@itemx usage.bad_cursor
@itemx usage.bad_target
An argument is missing or malformed, or names something the verb cannot
act on (the frame's only tab, a perspective persp-mode does not manage).
@item not_found.workspace
@itemx not_found.pane
Nothing at that name or address.
@item conflict.exists
@itemx conflict.kind_mismatch
@itemx conflict.already_managed
The name is taken, the key exists with another kind, or the workspace
to adopt is already tagged.
@item capability.unavailable
@itemx capability.unsupported_kind
@itemx capability.disabled
The backend, page, shell backend or verb is not available here.
@item pane.not_terminal
@code{send} to a pane with no terminal or process.
@item wait.timeout
The condition did not hold in time.
@item driver.unreachable
No Emacs server answered at the socket.
@item transport.timeout
@itemx transport.protocol
@itemx transport.emacs_error
The server answered too slowly (retryable), garbled, or with an error of
its own.
@item install.failed
@code{install-elisp} could not write the package.
@item internal.error
A handler bug; the message says what signaled.
@end table

@noindent
Warnings you may see: @samp{capture.cursor_reset}, @samp{layout.deferred},
@samp{layout.failed}, @samp{degraded.region_top},
@samp{degraded.layout_unavailable}, @samp{degraded.eat_unavailable},
@samp{eval.escape_hatch}, @samp{doctor.@var{check}},
@samp{flag.ignored} and @samp{flag.deprecated}.

@node Global Flags
@chapter Global Flags
@cindex global flags
@cindex pwm, shared flags

These flags are accepted anywhere on the command line.  They are one
flag table, shared with etm's sibling CLI @command{pwm}, which drives
tmux and Emacs together and speaks the same envelope shape.  Both list
the table as @code{global_flags} in @samp{capabilities}.  The spellings
are @command{ntm}'s.

@table @option
@item --json
The JSON envelope.  The default when standard output is not a terminal.
@item --robot-format=json|toon|markdown|text
@cindex TOON
@code{toon} states a uniform list once as @samp{key[@var{n}]@{a,b@}:}
plus comma-separated rows; @code{markdown} renders lists as tables;
@code{text} is the human view, the default on a terminal.
@item --robot-markdown
The same as @option{--robot-format=markdown}.
@item --fields @var{a},@var{b}
Keep only these keys of @code{data} (of each item, when @code{data} is a
list).
@item --brief
Summary only: every list in @code{data} becomes its count.
@item --verbose
Trace the parsed flags and the timing to standard error.
@item --no-color
No ANSI color.  None is emitted anyway, and @env{NO_COLOR} is honored.
@end table

@cindex modifiers
@cindex paging
The next five are @dfn{modifiers}: a verb without the notion answers
with a @samp{flag.ignored} warning instead of failing, and each verb's
@code{modifiers} in @samp{etm capabilities} say which it honors.

@table @option
@item --limit @var{n}
@itemx --offset @var{n}
Page a list verb (@code{ws ls}, @code{pane ls}, @code{events});
aliases @option{--robot-limit} and @option{--robot-offset}.  A paged
answer's @code{data} is @code{@{"items": [@dots{}], "pagination":
@{limit, offset, count, total, has_more@}, "next_offset": @var{n}@}},
@code{next_offset} being @code{null} on the last page.  Without them,
@code{data} stays the bare list.  @code{events} pages from the newest
end.
@item --since @var{t}
@code{events}: a time (RFC 3339, epoch seconds, or an age like
@samp{10m}) or an event id.  @code{capture} and @code{wait}: a cursor.
@item --timeout @var{dur}
@code{wait}: seconds, or @samp{500ms}, @samp{30s}, @samp{5m}, @samp{1h}.
@item --dry-run
Print the request that would be sent, without contacting Emacs.
@end table

The old spellings still work, with a @samp{flag.deprecated} warning
naming the new one: @option{--format json} (@option{--json}),
@option{--format compact} (@option{--robot-format=toon}),
@option{--format text} and @option{--format pretty}
(@option{--robot-format=text}).

@cindex transport flags
etm's own flags pick the Emacs and the transport:

@table @option
@item --socket @var{name-or-path}
@itemx -s @var{name-or-path}
The server socket.  Default: @env{ETM_SOCKET}, else
@env{EMACS_SOCKET_NAME}, else @samp{server}.
@item --transport auto|socket|emacsclient
@code{socket} speaks the server protocol directly; @code{emacsclient}
spawns @command{emacsclient --eval}; @code{auto} (the default) tries the
socket first.
@item --emacsclient @var{path}
The @command{emacsclient} the fallback runs.
@item --rpc-el @var{path}
The elisp to autoload (@pxref{The CLI}).
@item --no-autoload
Never load etm into Emacs.
@item --io-timeout @var{s}
One round trip's budget (default 30 seconds), for the whole exchange,
not each read.
@end table

@node Wire Protocol
@chapter Wire Protocol
@cindex wire protocol
@cindex protocol

Any client that can reach the Emacs server can speak etm; the CLI is one
such client.

@cindex request
A @dfn{request} is UTF-8 JSON:

@example
@{"v": 1, "verb": "pane.new",
 "args": @{"ws": "review", "key": "sh", "kind": "shell"@}@}
@end example

@noindent
It is sent base64-wrapped as the form @code{(etm-rpc
"@var{base64}")}, and the reply is base64 of the envelope, so neither
direction ever carries an Emacs Lisp string that needs quoting, and the
reply survives the server's framing byte for byte.

@cindex etm-rpc-unloaded
When @code{etm-rpc} is not defined, the form answers the symbol
@code{etm-rpc-unloaded}; the CLI then resends it with a @code{load} in
front (@pxref{The CLI}), with a budget of at least 30 seconds, because a
first load into a large configuration can be slow.  An Emacs that has
already loaded etm costs no extra round trip.

@cindex cursor format
A capture @dfn{cursor} is @samp{c1:@var{buffer-id}:@var{position}}: the
buffer's stable id (@code{etm-resource-buffer-id}) and a character
position.  Treat it as opaque, and pass it back exactly as received.

@defun etm-parse-addr addr
Split a pane address into @code{(@var{workspace} . @var{key})}, on the
@emph{last} slash, so workspace names may themselves contain one when
they did not come from @code{ws new}.  @var{workspace} is @code{nil} for
a bare key (the current workspace).  Keys match
@samp{[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._:+-]*}.
@end defun

@defun etm-cursor-format buffer-id pos
@defunx etm-cursor-parse cursor
Build and parse capture cursors.
@end defun

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting
@cindex troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item Exit 5, @samp{driver.unreachable}
@cindex unreachable server
No server answered at the socket.  Start one (@kbd{M-x server-start} or
@samp{emacs --daemon}) and check the name: @samp{etm --socket
@var{name} doctor}.  A daemon started as @samp{emacs --daemon=foo}
listens at @samp{foo}.

@item The first call is slow
@cindex first call
@cindex load time
The target Emacs had not loaded etm, so the first request loaded it,
which in a large configuration can take seconds.  Later calls do not
pay it.  To remove the wait, load etm at startup:

@lisp
(add-hook 'emacs-startup-hook (lambda () (require 'etm)))
@end lisp

@noindent
A caller with a short budget of its own should allow for this, or call
@samp{etm doctor} once as a warm-up.

@item @samp{transport.timeout}
@cindex slow verbs
Emacs answered nothing within @option{--io-timeout}.  The server runs
requests one at a time inside Emacs's command loop, so a busy or
prompting Emacs answers late.  Look for a minibuffer prompt in the
target frame.  The error is retryable.

@item A page pane stays @code{rendering}, or Emacs stops answering while one opens
@cindex slow pages
@cindex page stuck
Pages render on a timer, after @code{pane.new} has answered, but a
render that blocks still blocks Emacs while it runs.  @samp{etm doctor}
names the culprit: @samp{etm.pages} lists pages that failed or are still
rendering after @code{etm-page-stuck-seconds}, and
@samp{etm.pages-responsive} lists pages whose render took longer than
@code{etm-page-slow-ms}.  Make such a page asynchronous (draw first,
fetch on a timer), or give a page that prompts its arguments through
@code{etm-page-args} (@pxref{Page Types}).

@item @samp{os error 22} (EINVAL) on macOS
@cindex macOS
@cindex EINVAL
macOS refuses to set a read timeout on a socket whose peer has already
closed it, and Emacs answers and hangs up quickly.  etm treats that
EINVAL as ``the reply is complete'' and reads the rest, so the error
should never reach you.  If it does, you are running an older etm;
upgrade, or pass @option{--transport emacsclient} meanwhile.

@item @code{ws switch} fails under persp-mode in a daemon
persp-mode ignores the daemon's own frame.  Open a client frame
(@samp{emacsclient -t} or @samp{-c}) first.

@item @code{ws new} fails with @samp{capability.unavailable}
The @code{single} backend is in effect: turn on @code{tab-bar-mode},
@code{tabspaces-mode} or @code{persp-mode}, or set
@code{etm-workspace-backend}.  @samp{etm capabilities} names the
backend in @code{workspaces}.

@item p95 round trips of 15--20 ms in @samp{emacs -Q}
@cindex garbage collection
About one call in eight triggers a garbage collection under @samp{-Q}'s
small @code{gc-cons-threshold}.  A normal configuration's larger
threshold brings p95 near p50 (about 1.5 ms).  Measure with
@samp{etm bench}.
@end table

